Pillar One-line meaning In the code below Encapsulation Keep data and the methods that guard it together; hide the internals <code>Account._balance</code> can only change through <code>deposit()</code> Abstraction Show what an object does, hide how <code>Shape</code> says every shape has an <code>area()</code>, without saying how Inheritance A class reuses and extends another class <code>Circle</code> and <code>Rectangle</code> are both a <code>Shape</code> Polymorphism The same call behaves differently depending on the object <code>shape.area()</code> runs different code for each shape <pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod


class Shape(ABC):                     # abstraction: a contract, not a real shape
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    @abstractmethod
    def area(self):
        pass

    def describe(self):
        return f"{self.name} with area {self.area():.2f}"


class Rectangle(Shape):               # inheritance
    def __init__(self, length, width):
        super().__init__("Rectangle")
        self.length = length
        self.width = width

    def area(self):
        return self.length * self.width


class Circle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, radius):
        super().__init__("Circle")
        self.radius = radius

    def area(self):
        return 3.14159 * self.radius ** 2


for shape in [Rectangle(4, 5), Circle(1)]:
    print(shape.describe())           # polymorphism: same call, different area()
</code></pre> <pre><code>Rectangle with area 20.00
Circle with area 3.14
</code></pre> <p><code>Shape("x")</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>, because an abstract class cannot be instantiated.</p>
